SPU: single-precision mode RC_SPU_XFLOAT=1 (round toward zero, FTZ, DAZ on SPU host threads); helpers exact under it.

Problem: the SPU single-precision unit truncates, reads denorm operands as zero, gives +0 for denorm and zero results,
and has no Inf/NaN (SPU ISA v1.2 s.9.1 p.195, fma p.208, fi p.219) [src:spu-isa-1.2]. The helpers use the host's SSE ops
under the default MXCSR (round to nearest, no flush) and only recompute exponent-255 lanes exactly (KZ3 1071/1079/1089):
ordinary lanes round up to 1 ulp above the SPU, keep denormals (fcgt(0, -denorm) true where the SPU says false; cflts of
a denormal 2^24 instead of 0), and return -0 (fm of a zero by a negative operand, fma family, FTZ underflow)
(the RE notes s.25, oracle 6506). RPCS3, on which these games work with "SPU XFloat Accuracy: Approximate", sets round
toward zero and zeroing denormals on every SPU thread (spu_thread::cpu_task, read for behaviour, nothing copied)
[src:rpcs3-sputhread]. Nexus 1.01 prison_transport falls through the floor in the port; its collision jobs
(igCollBroadSpu 0x00C21400, igcollspu 0x00C2D800, the RE notes s.36) are float code: fm fma fa fs fcgt fnms fms fi
frsqest frest fcmgt cuflt csflt fceq. A host-rounding or denormal difference changes their compare outcomes at exact
boundaries (a point on a plane, a shared triangle edge), which a lifted-vs-interpreted differential cannot see (both
use these helpers).

Fix (runtime/spu/spu_fpmode.h, spu_drain.c, spu_helpers.h):
- RC_SPU_XFLOAT=1: spu_host_fp_init (SPURS kernel/lane/PM/jobchain threads) and spu_fpmode_init_thread (raw SPU,
  workload task threads) set MXCSR round toward zero | FTZ | DAZ (0xE040), and log "[spu-fp] RC_SPU_XFLOAT=1: SPU host
  thread MXCSR FFC0 ..." once per starting module. Unset/0 = as before (A/B switch). SPU code that a PPU thread runs
  synchronously (spu_workload_dispatch_job / spu_run_spurs_job called on the pushing thread) keeps that thread's MXCSR.
- Result-range lanes: spu__xr_mask_r (|r| >= 0x7F7FFFFF) replaces the exponent-255 result test of fa/fs/fm, csflt/cuflt
  and the fma reference path; the -mfma fma fast path's odd test also sends 0x7F7FFFFF. Under truncation an x86 overflow
  is +-FLT_MAX, not Inf, so the old test missed it; those lanes now take the exact lane code (same cost: cmpgt for cmpeq).
- +0 results: spu__pos0 (lane == 0x80000000 -> 0) on fa/fs/fm/fma/fms/fnms/csflt, all modes.
- fi: every lane in the integer formulation (spu__fi_int4 / spu__fi_lane, KZ3 1134 / PR #287 lane code), not only the
  exponent-255 lanes: the float form rounded to nearest and, under FTZ|DAZ, flushed step*y (frest+fi down to 4 bits for
  x exponent 242..249). The float form stays for tests (spu__fi_ref, spu__fi_float4).
- fma reference path (cold): in the SPU mode every lane from spu__fma_lane (no libm fmaf, whose rounding-mode behaviour
  on MinGW is not known).
- Double-precision ops (dfa dfs dfm dfma dfms dfnms dfnma, frds): the SPU rounds them per FPSCR (nearest after reset,
  s.9.2), so under the SPU mode they run with the default MXCSR between two MXCSR writes (operands and results pinned by
  empty asm statements), then restore it. Unchanged in the default mode.

Tests: runtime/spu/tests/test_spu_xfloat_mode.c (tools/spu/run_spu_xfloat_test.sh): an integer reference of ISA chapter 9
written for this patch, 17 op families on all edge pairs/triples (36 words x 2 signs) and random tuples, strict in the
RC_SPU_XFLOAT=1 mode: 0 mismatches on 9.0 M lanes, nofma and -mfma; double ops round to nearest, MXCSR control restored.
Negative control (the tree without this patch, MXCSR set by hand): FAIL (fa 3240, fm 44035, fma 54622, fi(frest) 19320,
fi(any) 27657 mismatches: zero sign, range (FLT_MAX for an overflow), fi rounding; frest+fi 4 bits; double ops 10 lanes).
The compares and cflts/cfltu already match with the MXCSR alone (DAZ). The 6506 oracle gets a 'spu' mode (MXCSR 0x7FC0): 0 mismatches in every
single-precision family on 1.0-7.3 M lanes each (was fa 28425, fm 297183, fma 171671, csflt 1813352 ... with the
same MXCSR before the patch). fesd/frds keep their known exp-255 widening (not used by the collision jobs).

ASSUMPTION: the real SPU's fi equals the ISA formula truncated (as RPCS3's integer fi); the estimate tables (frest,
frsqest) are unchanged.
diff --git a/runtime/spu/spu_fpmode.h b/runtime/spu/spu_fpmode.h
index 2ea5fae..cdbfc86 100644
--- a/runtime/spu/spu_fpmode.h
+++ b/runtime/spu/spu_fpmode.h
@@ -20,7 +20,33 @@
 #define SPU_FPMODE_X86 1
 #endif
 
+#include <stdlib.h>
 #define SPU_FPMODE_FTZ_DAZ 0x8040u
+/* RC patch 7107: the SPU single-precision mode, MXCSR round toward zero (RC = 11, 0x6000) | FTZ | DAZ.
+ * SPU ISA v1.2 s.9.1 (p.195): "The only supported rounding mode is truncation (toward zero)", denorm operands read as
+ * zero, a would-be denorm result is +0. RPCS3 sets the same host state on every SPU thread (spu_thread::cpu_task:
+ * fesetround(FE_TOWARDZERO) and zeroing denormals; read for behaviour, nothing copied) [src:rpcs3-sputhread]. The helpers
+ * keep the extended range exact under it (spu__xr_mask_r: a truncated overflow is +-FLT_MAX), give +0 for zero
+ * results (spu__pos0), compute fi in integers, and run the double-precision ops under the default MXCSR (spu_helpers.h).
+ * On by RC_SPU_XFLOAT=1 (value starting with '1'); unset or 0 = the host's default rounding as before (KZ3_SPU_FTZ=1
+ * still sets FTZ|DAZ alone). */
+#define SPU_FPMODE_SPU 0xE040u
+static inline int spu_fpmode_rc_on(void)
+{
+    static int s_on = -1;
+    if (s_on < 0) { const char* e = getenv("RC_SPU_XFLOAT"); s_on = (e && *e == '1') ? 1 : 0; }
+    return s_on;
+}
+#include <stdio.h>
+static inline void spu_fpmode_set_spu_rz(void)
+{
+#ifdef SPU_FPMODE_X86
+    _mm_setcsr((_mm_getcsr() & ~0x6000u) | SPU_FPMODE_SPU);
+    static int s_said;   /* once per translation unit that starts SPU threads (drain, raw, workload) */
+    if (!s_said) { s_said = 1; fprintf(stderr, "[spu-fp] RC_SPU_XFLOAT=1: SPU host thread MXCSR %04X (round toward zero|FTZ|DAZ)\n",
+                                       _mm_getcsr() & ~0x3Fu); }
+#endif
+}
 
 /* KZ3_SPU_FTZ: on only when set to a value starting with '1' (opt-in, SPU pass 70: no throughput change measured). */
 static inline int spu_fpmode_env_on(const char* e) { return (e && *e == '1') ? 1 : 0; }
@@ -42,6 +68,7 @@ static inline void spu_fpmode_init_thread(void)
     static int s_on = -1;
     if (s_on < 0) s_on = spu_fpmode_env_on(getenv("KZ3_SPU_FTZ"));
     if (s_on) spu_fpmode_set_spu();
+    if (spu_fpmode_rc_on()) spu_fpmode_set_spu_rz();   /* RC patch 7107 */
 }
 
